  ]

  #sol-item(114, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* Light spreads over a sphere of area $4 pi d^2$, so the illuminance falls as the inverse square of the distance.
  ]

  #sol-item(115, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* $E = I\/d^2 = 200\/16 = 12.5$ lux.
  ]

  #sol-item(116, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* Coherent sources have the same frequency and a constant phase difference, so the fringes stay still.
  ]

  #sol-item(117, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* $lambda = a x \/ d$, with $a$ the slit separation, $x$ the fringe spacing and $d$ the distance to the screen.
  ]

  #sol-item(118, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* Only a transverse vibration can be restricted to one plane.
  ]

  #sol-item(119, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* A gap of about one wavelength spreads the wave appreciably; a wide gap barely spreads it at all.
  ]

  #sol-item(120, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("bank120", default: 1.5pt))[
    *Chapter 6.* Scattering is strongest for short wavelengths, so blue reaches the eye from every part of the sky.
  ]

